---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import corrections from "../data/corrections.json";

// These entries are hand-written HTML, so their hrefs need the site base
// prefixed. Open questions shipped five dead links by applying this to one
// field and not the other; this page then did the same thing, which is what
// tools/check_site.py now exists to catch.
const base = (html) => String(html || "").replace(/href='\//g, `href='${u("/")}`);

// Every claim this archive made and then withdrew. Kept in date order so the
// direction of travel is visible: nearly all of them are the archive becoming
// less certain, not more.
const C = [
  { d: "September 2026", t: "House 114 was called a household. It is a zadruga.",
    was: "That Smokvica Krmpotska <strong>kbr. 114</strong> identified one family, and so placed Juraj Blažević in Toma's household — nearly naming his father.",
    now: "A control document, the 1890 baptism of Frane Blažević, puts <strong>Pavao Blažević, son of Luka</strong>, at 114 as well. Luka and Toma's father Anton are both sons of Ilija, so Pavao and Toma are <strong>first cousins</strong> and the number covers an extended-family compound — a <strong>zadruga</strong>.",
    cost: "Question 1b was downgraded from critical to substantive. The house places Juraj in Ilija's kin-group; it cannot name his father.",
    href: "/blazevic" },

  { d: "September 2026", t: "Zvonimir Vukelić was not a casualty-list death.",
    was: "That he should be looked for in the Austro-Hungarian <em>Verlustlisten</em>, reasoning from his age — thirty-two in the second year of the war.",
    now: "The tree places his death <strong>at Senj</strong> and gives his occupation as <em>gostioničar, bankar</em>. A man who dies at home in a settled trade is not a casualty-list entry.",
    cost: "The right book is the <strong>Senj death register for 1915</strong>. The reasoning was plausible and it was still wrong: an age is not a cause.",
    href: "/open-questions" },

  { d: "September 2026", t: "The Karlobag material was filed under the wrong family.",
    was: "A block on Karlobag's merchant milieu placed on the <strong>Žubrinić</strong> page.",
    now: "It arrives through <strong>Matija Pilipić</strong>, ahnentafel 13 — the <strong>Papić</strong> line. Moved.",
    cost: "Nothing to the evidence; everything to whether a reader can follow the line it belongs to.",
    href: "/papic" },

  { d: "September 2026", t: "Two letters went to the wrong archive.",
    was: "Both drafted to <strong>Državni arhiv u Gospiću</strong>, on the assumption that Lika-Senj county means Gospić.",
    now: "Gospić's remit is Lika-Senj <em>“osim gradova Senja i Novalje”</em> — <strong>except the towns of Senj and Novalja</strong>. And Smokvica is in Primorje-Gorski Kotar, not Lika-Senj at all. Both belong to <strong>Rijeka, Senj branch</strong>.",
    cost: "Had they been sent, two archives would have spent time forwarding them. Jurisdiction is now written down before anything is posted.",
    href: "/sources" },

  { d: "September 2026", t: "The search did not fold given names.",
    was: "That folding <em>Žubrinić / Xubrinich / Zubrinich</em> together was enough to make a person findable.",
    now: "It was not. <em>Ivan</em> found 42 of 88 Ivans, because the rest are entered <em>Joannes</em>, <em>Joannis</em>, <em>Juan</em> or <em>Ive</em>. <strong>268 canonical given names, 34 of them under more than one spelling.</strong>",
    cost: "Caught by the reader, not by this archive. Both surnames and given names now fold — and the split by sex was a second correction on top, after <em>Franjo</em> and <em>Franciska</em> were wrongly merged.",
    href: "/people" },

  { d: "September 2026", t: "Forty Latin case-endings were blamed on a transcriber.",
    was: "That <em>Josephum</em>, <em>Joannis</em> and <em>Mariam</em> were <strong>one person</strong> working through Selce and Vinodol entries and lifting the word after <em>baptizavi</em>.",
    now: "Three scans, two parishes, three priests, three decades apart — <strong>the priests wrote the oblique forms themselves</strong>. Michaël Kombol writes <em>nomine</em> + accusative for every child on the 1806 page.",
    cost: "The artefact is real; the culprit was invented. It also turned the ending into a <em>useful</em> signal — accusative means the entry is the person's own, genitive means they were only named as somebody's parent.",
    href: "/name" },

  { d: "September 2026", t: "A name was called resolved on the strength of a sorting bucket.",
    was: "That <strong>Luca “Papić or Perpic”</strong> was settled, because her siblings are recorded Papić.",
    now: "Her father is a record called <em class='mono'>“Senj — Unknown To be Sorted Papić”</em>. Those siblings were grouped by whoever compiled the tree, not by a register.",
    cost: "The tooling now reuses the same sorting-bucket filter the rest of the build uses, so a bucket can never resolve a name. Marked open.",
    href: "/name" },

  { d: "September 2026", t: "Her surname is Gergacs, not Gerkacs.",
    was: "<strong>Mariam Gerkacs (Gergacs)</strong>, ahnentafel 55, following the tree's ordering.",
    now: "The Karlobag register of August 1789 spells it <strong>Gergacs</strong>. The tree leads with the secondary form.",
    cost: "Small, and worth doing: the register's spelling is the one an archivist will search on.",
    href: "/people/mariam-gerkacs-gergacs" },

  { d: "September 2026", t: "A misreading of my own, corrected at higher resolution.",
    was: "That the 1918 marriage entry for Ana Papić gave her mother as <em>Antonija r. <strong>Papić</strong></em> — a clerk confusing the two family names.",
    now: "Enlarged, it plainly reads <strong>Prpić</strong>. There was no clerk's error.",
    cost: "Nothing, because it was caught before it was published as a finding. Recorded because a correction that never reached the page is still a correction.",
    href: "/prpic" },

  { d: "September 2026", t: "Five links were built without the site's base path.",
    was: "Open questions rewrote <code class='mono'>href='/…'</code> in the question body but not in the <em>“what would settle it”</em> block.",
    now: "Both fields go through one helper. Two of the five had been dead since the page was written.",
    cost: "A reader clicking the most actionable part of the page got a 404. Found by sweeping the built site rather than the source.",
    href: "/open-questions" },

  { d: "September 2026", t: "A second wife, or a second man — the Orešković join gets worse.",
    was: "That the wrong-attachment problem was contained: a real <a href='/people/maria-catharina-oreskovic-oreskovic'>Maria Catharina Orešković</a> of Dubrava had been married to the wrong man, and the rest of the line stood.",
    now: "Reading the <strong>1845</strong> Otočac baptism of Josephus gives the mother as <strong>Maria Žubrinić <em>nata Marković</em></strong>, where the 1834 baptism of Nicolaus gives <strong>Maria <em>nata Orešković</em></strong>. Either the first wife died and he married a second Maria, or <strong>there are two men of this name</strong> — there are 68 Žubrinić records at Otočac.",
    cost: "The archive had already established that Maria Catharina was the wrong woman — that reversal is set out on <a href='/frontier'>the Frontier page</a> and is not new here. What is new is that the <em>husband</em> is now uncertain too, which is a larger problem than the one it replaces.",
    href: "/open-questions" },

  { d: "September 2026", t: "I blamed the download for four thumbnails. It was the upload.",
    was: "Question 5c, published this morning, said four key scans had been <em>downloaded</em> from MyHeritage at the wrong size and should simply be re-fetched — <q>no archive, no letter, no travel</q>.",
    now: "Testing it took two minutes and disproved it. The MyHeritage CDN returns <strong>exactly 960 pixels</strong> for the 1885 marriage, so the file held in the tree is itself 960 pixels. What separates the good scans is <strong>where they came from</strong>: every 4,500-pixel document carries a <strong>FamilySearch image identifier</strong> in its filename, and no 960-pixel one does.",
    cost: "The wrong fix. Re-fetching would have returned the same four thumbnails and the question would have looked answered. The real action is to re-source three entries from FamilySearch — and the registers and pages for all three are already known.",
    href: "/open-questions" },

  { d: "September 2026", t: "A Karlobag baptism was attached to the wrong Caetano Bacchi.",
    was: "<a href='/people/caetano-bacchi'>Caetano Bacchi</a>, ahnentafel 214, born <strong>12 January 1779</strong> — carried from the tree, whose own note reads <span class='mono'>“(Maybe)”</span>.",
    now: "The 1779 Karlobag entry is real, but ahn 214's daughter died in 1795 and <em>her</em> daughter was born <strong>11 March 1781</strong>. <strong>Two years cannot hold two generations.</strong> The right man belongs around 1730–1745.",
    cost: "Two more ancestors go with it: <strong>Angelo Bacchi</strong> and <strong>Catharina</strong> are in this pedigree only because that entry names them. Marked disputed, not deleted — the wrong join is the only pointer to where the right man should be sought.",
    href: "/impossible" },

  { d: "September 2026", t: "The Frontier colonel's name was misread as Ballinich.",
    was: "The 1777 Uroda godfather given as <em>Pro-Colonellus Joannes <strong>Ballinich</strong> de Zirfeld</em>.",
    now: "The same surname recurs in a February 1779 entry where the words <em>Joës <strong>Bapta</strong></em> sit on the same line — giving a clean two-bowled capital <strong>B</strong> to compare against. The surname's initial is a single looped ascender with a leg, and plainly not that letter. It is <strong>Hallinich de Zirfeld</strong>.",
    cost: "Small in itself, and the method is the point: a contested letter was settled by finding the candidate letter elsewhere in the same hand, rather than by staring harder at the word.",
    href: "/frontier" },

  { d: "September 2026", t: "Four sorting buckets were published as ancestors.",
    was: "<span class='mono'>1800-1830 Birth — Selce Papić (Papa)</span>, two more like it, and <span class='mono'>1700 Birth — Selce Papić</span>, all with person pages and children attached.",
    now: "They are <strong>research scaffolding</strong>, not people. The build has always excluded such records — the code comment says publishing them <em>“would be a lie”</em> — but the filter did not recognise a name opening with a year, nor <em>Brothers of Casparus (It Seems)</em>.",
    cost: "Four people who never existed had pages, and were parents in other people's family lists. The person count falls from 1,090 to <strong>1,086</strong>. Found not by reading but by the new <a href='/impossible'>self-consistency check</a>: a bucket dated 1800-1830 cannot father someone born in 1824.",
    href: "/impossible" },

  { d: "September 2026", t: "A direct ancestor was recorded as dying eight years before her wedding.",
    was: "<a href='/people/tereza-pecanic'>Tereza Pećanić</a>, ahnentafel 9, carried through from the tree as <strong>died before 1850</strong> — published without anyone checking it against the rest of her own record.",
    now: "The same record marries her to <a href='/people/toma-tomislav-blazevic'>Toma</a> on <strong>21 November 1858</strong> and gives her children in <strong>1860, 1880, 1882 and 1884</strong>. Toma's death entry of 26 July 1907 calls him <em>udovac iza pok. Tereze</em> — widower of the late Tereza. So she was alive in 1884 and dead by <strong>July 1907</strong>.",
    cost: "Nothing downstream — no date was computed from it. But it sat on a direct ancestor's page for the life of this archive, and it is the kind of error that needs no register to catch: <strong>her own children postdate her death by up to thirty-four years</strong>. The lesson is that internal consistency was never checked, only external agreement.",
    href: "/people/tereza-pecanic" },

  { d: "September 2026", t: "Five stock graphics were published as portraits of ancestors.",
    was: "Five items in the media library shown as <strong>photographs</strong> of Michael Žubrinić (b. 1811), Maria Catharina Orešković (1835), Josephum Antić (1806), Maria Jelličić (1809) and Georgius Antić Papić (1838).",
    now: "They are <strong>stock question-mark placeholder graphics</strong>, uploaded to the tree for people who have no picture. One still carries a visible <strong>Adobe Stock watermark</strong>. The dates alone should have caught it: a photograph of a man born in 1806 would be remarkable.",
    cost: "They were on the pictures page and on two person pages, captioned as those people. The media build now honours an <span class='mono'>excluded</span> classification and drops them. A sixth was reclassified rather than dropped — a picture captioned <em>“Ljubomir Blazevic”</em> is the family gravestone, not a portrait.",
    href: "/faces" },

  { d: "September 2026", t: "The gazetteer was counting one village as several.",
    was: "Otočac shown with 60 Žubrinić records; four Mrzli Dol records filed under a place called <em class='mono'>“#6 Mrzli Dol”</em>.",
    now: "<em>Otocac Croatia</em> is <em>Otočac</em> — 68 records. The house-number pattern missed <code class='mono'>#</code> and <code class='mono'>kbr.</code>, hiding houses 9 and 30 entirely.",
    cost: "Every count on the map page was low. Fixed when the tool was widened from one surname to four.",
    href: "/gazetteer" },
];

const kinds = {
  corrected: "Corrected", note: "Noted", confirmed: "Confirmed", disputed: "Disputed",
};
---
<Base title="What Changed"
  eyebrow="Eighteen things this archive got wrong, and what fixed them"
  dek="An archive that never contradicts itself is not being careful; it is not checking. This is the list of claims made here and then withdrawn, kept in the open because the reasoning that failed is as useful as the reasoning that held.">

  <section class="blk">
    <p class="lede">Every page here carries a confidence label, and a label is only honest if it can
    be <em>lowered</em>. Most of what follows is this archive becoming <strong>less</strong>
    certain, not more: a house number demoted from an identifier to a kin-group, a resolved name
    reopened, a culprit un-named. <strong>One was caught by the reader</strong> — the search had
    been folding surnames and not given names, and was finding 42 of 88 Ivans. One was caught by
    sweeping the <em>built</em> site rather than the source, which is a lesson about where to look.</p>
  </section>

  {C.map((c, i) => (
    <section class="blk fix">
      <div class="lab">{c.d}</div>
      <h2><span class="mono dim">{i + 1}.</span> {c.t}</h2>
      <table class="kv" style="margin-top:.8rem">
        <tbody>
          <tr><th>This archive said</th><td set:html={base(c.was)} /></tr>
          <tr><th>The record says</th><td set:html={base(c.now)} /></tr>
          <tr><th>What it cost</th><td set:html={base(c.cost)} /></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p style="margin-top:.8rem"><a class="btn" href={u(c.href)}>Where it now stands →</a></p>
    </section>
  ))}

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>And the record-level corrections</h2>
    <p class="lede">These are narrower — a date, a spelling, a single person — and each is attached
    to the person it concerns, so a reader who never opens this page still meets it.</p>
    <table class="reg" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <thead><tr><th>What</th><th>The tree</th><th>The record</th><th></th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{corrections.map((r) => (
        <tr>
          <td><span class="tag">{kinds[r.kind] || r.kind}</span> {r.what}</td>
          <td class="dim" set:html={base(r.tree)} />
          <td set:html={base(r.record)} />
          <td>{r.href && <a href={u(r.href)}>→</a>}</td>
        </tr>))}</tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What is deliberately still wrong</h2>
    <p class="lede">Two things on this site are known to be inconsistent and have been left that way
    on purpose, because tidying them would destroy the evidence that they conflict.</p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li style="margin-bottom:.9rem"><strong>An ancestral branch attached to the wrong woman.</strong>
        Now that the 1834 register has been read, <a href={u("/people/maria-catharina-oreskovic-oreskovic")}>Maria
        Catharina Orešković</a> is known <em>not</em> to be Nicolaus's mother — but the archive
        cannot say who the right Maria Orešković was, so the branch is left attached and marked
        rather than severed into a dead end. Cutting it would destroy the only pointer anyone has
        to where the real woman should be looked for.</li>
      <li style="margin-bottom:.9rem"><strong>Two sisters born eighteen days apart.</strong>
        Franjka Papić, 20 January 1895, and Šulka Papić, 7 February 1895, of the same parents —
        each date written in that woman's own marriage register. The tree reproduces both
        faithfully, so the disagreement is between the <em>records</em>.
        <a href={u("/open-questions")}>Question 5b →</a></li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:1rem">The rule is the same in both cases: <strong>reproduce the
    contradiction, attribute it, and say what would settle it.</strong> An archive that resolves a
    conflict it cannot actually resolve has replaced a record with an opinion.
    <a href={u("/method")}>Method →</a></p>
  </section>
</Base>
